1
00:00:00,116 --> 00:00:10,025
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:15,444 --> 00:00:18,744
So let's take a deeper look
at these lifecycle methods.

3
00:00:18,744 --> 00:00:22,470
The first method that
gets called is OnCreate.

4
00:00:22,470 --> 00:00:26,400
OnCreate gets called when
the activity is first created.

5
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:29,750
Typically OnCreate is used to
initialize the activity and

6
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:32,740
will carry out the following
four functions.

7
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:37,260
It will call super.onCreate so Android
can do some of its own initialization.

8
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:39,806
It will set the activity's content view,

9
00:00:39,806 --> 00:00:43,920
which tells Android what the activity's
user interface should be.

10
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:48,930
Next, it will capture and
retain any references it needs to

11
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:52,930
the various views or
elements of the user interface.

12
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:58,040
And finally, it will configure the user
interface views and elements as required.

13
00:00:58,040 --> 00:01:01,230
So, let's take a look at
MapLocation's onCreate method.

14
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:06,630
Here I've opened up the MapLocation
application in Eclipse.

15
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:10,520
And now,
I'll open up the MapLocation activity.

16
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:18,404
And here, as you can see, the MapLocation
class is a subclass of Activity and

17
00:01:18,404 --> 00:01:21,278
it overrides Activity.onCreate.

18
00:01:21,278 --> 00:01:25,700
Inside the onCreate method, you see
the four functions we just talked about.

19
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:30,020
First, there's a call to super.onCreate.

20
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:32,980
Next, there's a call to setContentView,

21
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:37,380
passing in the ID of a layout
file as a parameter.

22
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:42,160
After that, the activity stores
references to an EditText view and

23
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:45,050
to a button which appear
in the user interface.

24
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:49,880
And finally, onCreate attaches
a listener to the button.

25
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,430
Android calls this listener code every
time the user presses this button.

26
00:01:56,430 --> 00:02:01,020
So, this listener code is listening,
for presses of the button.

27
00:02:03,215 --> 00:02:05,780
OnRestart is another lifecycle method.

28
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:11,090
It gets called if the activity has been
stopped, but is about to be started again.

29
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:15,840
And you can use onRestart to handle any
special processing that you need, that, or

30
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:19,720
that is needed only after the activity's
been stopped, but is about to start again.

31
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:25,030
onStart is called when the activity
is about to become visible.

32
00:02:25,030 --> 00:02:29,875
And some typical applications for
this method include starting when

33
00:02:29,875 --> 00:02:35,140
visible-only behaviors, like
requesting location sensor updates, or

34
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:38,985
loading or
resetting persistent application state,

35
00:02:38,985 --> 00:02:44,770
things like updating the unread messages
queue for an email reader application.

36
00:02:44,770 --> 00:02:47,863
The onResume method gets called
when the activity is visible and

37
00:02:47,863 --> 00:02:49,955
about to start interacting with the user.

38
00:02:49,955 --> 00:02:55,300
And some things you do in this method
are to start foreground-only behaviors,

39
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:59,270
things like starting animations or
playing a background sound track.

40
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:04,540
onPause is called when your
activity is about to lose focus.

41
00:03:04,540 --> 00:03:05,110
In this method,

42
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:10,590
you can shut down foreground-only
behaviors, things like killing animations.

43
00:03:10,590 --> 00:03:13,940
And you should also save any persistent
state that the user has edited.

44
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:19,370
onStop is called when the activity
is no longer visible to the user.

45
00:03:19,370 --> 00:03:23,160
At this point, it's possible that
the activity may be restarted later.

46
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:27,220
So, some typical things to do here
include caching activity state that

47
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:33,050
you'll want to restore when the activity
is later restarting and onStart is called.

48
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:37,750
And remember, onStop is not always
called when an activity terminates.

49
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:41,520
For instance, it may not be called
if Android kills the application's

50
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,410
process due to low memory.

51
00:03:44,410 --> 00:03:47,100
So don't wait to save
persistent data in this method.

52
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:48,620
Go do that back in onPause.

53
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:54,350
onDestroy is called with the activity
is about to be destroyed and

54
00:03:54,350 --> 00:03:59,150
some typical actions to do here
include releasing activity resources.

55
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:03,670
For example, shutting down private threads
that were started by this activity.

56
00:04:03,670 --> 00:04:07,010
And again, remember,
onDestroy may not be called at all.

57
00:04:07,010 --> 00:04:10,160
For example, when Android kills
an application due to low memory on

58
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:10,775
the device.

59
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:20,938
So, returning back to MapLocation,
you'll see that I've also overridden

60
00:04:20,938 --> 00:04:26,718
most of the activity lifecycle methods,
including onStart,

61
00:04:26,718 --> 00:04:32,410
onRestart, onResume, onPause,
onStop, and onDestroy.

62
00:04:32,410 --> 00:04:37,080
In each of these methods, you'll see that
I placed a method call that logs some

63
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:40,320
information about the method being called.

64
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:45,190
And I encourage you to run
the MapLocation application yourself and

65
00:04:45,190 --> 00:04:49,260
to watch the logcat view to see
exactly what's being written.

66
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:52,950
And I want you to try pressing different
buttons like the Back button and

67
00:04:52,950 --> 00:04:57,020
the Home button, and
different keys to verify for yourself that

68
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:02,360
the lifecycle method calls are following
the graphic depiction that we saw earlier.

69
00:05:04,350 --> 00:05:07,150
So now that we've seen
the activity lifecycle in action,

70
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:11,760
let's take a look at how one activity can
programatically start another activity.

71
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:15,880
To start an activity programatically,
you first create

72
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:20,678
an Intent object that specifies
the activity that you want to start.

73
00:05:21,910 --> 00:05:25,958
And then you pass this newly
created Intent to methods such as

74
00:05:25,958 --> 00:05:29,571
startActivity or startActivityForResult.

75
00:05:30,795 --> 00:05:33,750
startActivity will start
up the desired activity,

76
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:35,865
pushing the current activity
out of the foreground.

77
00:05:35,865 --> 00:05:41,530
startActivityForResult will also
start up the desired activity, but

78
00:05:41,530 --> 00:05:45,680
it will do so with the expectation
that the started activity will

79
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:49,980
provide a result back to
that calling activity.

80
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:54,030
And in this case, the result is
communicated back by a call to

81
00:05:54,030 --> 00:05:57,088
that activity's onActivityResult method.

82
00:05:58,320 --> 00:05:59,870
And we'll come back to
that in a few minutes.

83
00:06:00,930 --> 00:06:03,430
So, for now,
let's go back into Eclipse and

84
00:06:03,430 --> 00:06:08,370
look at how MapLocation actually
starts up the Google Maps activity.

85
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:15,220
Here I'm showing where MapLocation
has attached a listener to

86
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:17,210
the Show Map button.

87
