123
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:50,460
In particular, this code creates
a slightly different Intent and

124
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:54,795
then passes that Intent as a parameter
to startActivityForResult.

125
00:08:56,140 --> 00:08:59,410
We'll look at intents in more
detail in the next lesson.

126
00:08:59,410 --> 00:09:02,890
But you can also see here that
startActivityForResult takes

127
00:09:02,890 --> 00:09:06,750
a second parameter,
which represents a request code.

128
00:09:06,750 --> 00:09:10,600
And this will come in handy later
when the result is returned back to

129
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:11,740
the started activity.

130
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:17,580
Once the new activity is started,
it is responsible for

131
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:21,850
setting the result that should be
returned back to the calling activity.

132
00:09:21,850 --> 00:09:26,340
And it does this by calling
Activity.setResult, passing in

133
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:29,930
a result code and
optionally passing in some result data.

134
00:09:29,930 --> 00:09:35,860
Now, these result codes include some
built-in code such as RESULT_CANCELED,

135
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:39,790
which indicates that the user chose
not to complete the activity normally.

136
00:09:39,790 --> 00:09:41,592
They probably hit the Back button,
for example.

137
00:09:41,592 --> 00:09:46,780
And RESULT_OK, indicating that
the activity, in fact, completed normally.

138
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:51,569
Developers can also add
custom result codes after

139
00:09:51,569 --> 00:09:55,530
the built-in result code,
RESULT_FIRST_USER.

140
00:09:55,530 --> 00:09:58,870
Now, before the activity ends,
the activity must set its

141
00:09:58,870 --> 00:10:03,200
result by calling setResult and
this result will eventually be

142
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:09,670
transmitted back to the calling activity
via the call to onActivityResult.

143
00:10:09,670 --> 00:10:13,727
So let's take a look at that code in
the MapLocationFromContacts application.

144
00:10:15,510 --> 00:10:16,900
Here's onActivityResult.

145
00:10:18,050 --> 00:10:21,490
As you can see, its parameters include
the initial request code that was

146
00:10:21,490 --> 00:10:25,850
passed into startActivityForResult,
the result

147
00:10:25,850 --> 00:10:30,500
code used by the started activity
when it called setResult, and

148
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:35,455
an Intent object if it was passed
in when setResult was called.

149
00:10:35,455 --> 00:10:39,350
onActivityResult usually starts off
by checking the result code and

150
00:10:39,350 --> 00:10:44,390
request codes to determine what to
do with that particular result.

151
00:10:44,390 --> 00:10:49,040
In this example, the next steps are to
retrieve the underlying contact data and

152
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:53,185
to parse it to extract just
the postal address of the contact.

153
00:10:53,185 --> 00:10:57,190
Now, the details aren't important now,
so I'll skip over them.

154
00:10:57,190 --> 00:10:59,615
But once the postal address
has been extracted,

155
00:10:59,615 --> 00:11:03,797
MapLocationFromContacts operates
just like MapLocation did.

156
00:11:05,080 --> 00:11:10,248
It processes the data so that it's in a
format that Google Map expects, puts that

157
00:11:10,248 --> 00:11:15,649
data into an Intent object, and then call
startActivity, passing in that Intent.

158
00:11:18,498 --> 00:11:22,530
The last topic I'll discuss today
is handling configuration changes.

159
00:11:23,530 --> 00:11:26,270
A device's configuration
refers to device and

160
00:11:26,270 --> 00:11:31,990
application characteristics related
to application resources, things like

161
00:11:31,990 --> 00:11:38,280
languages used, screen size, keyboard
availability, and device orientation.

162
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:41,150
Now, these characteristics
can change at runtime.

163
00:11:41,150 --> 00:11:45,225
And when they do, Android will
usually kill the current activity and

164
00:11:45,225 --> 00:11:50,260
then restart it with the appropriate
resources for the changed configuration.

165
00:11:50,260 --> 00:11:53,280
Now, since configuration changes
can happen frequently while using

166
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:58,420
an application, you want to make sure
that your lifecycle method code is fast.

167
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:01,180
Consider device orientation changes,
for example.

168
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:06,060
If you move the device from portrait
mode to landscape mode, and back to

169
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:11,190
portrait mode, that will cause the current
activity to be killed and restarted twice.

170
00:12:12,250 --> 00:12:14,120
So if your startup code is slow,

171
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:18,520
that's really going to affect your
users' experience with your application.

172
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:22,500
So, to improve the speed of
configuration changes if you need it,

173
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:24,680
Android allows you to do two things.

174
00:12:24,680 --> 00:12:27,930
One, as the change is occurring,
you can create and save

175
00:12:27,930 --> 00:12:33,410
an arbitrary Java object that essentially
caches important state information.

176
00:12:33,410 --> 00:12:36,910
And two, you can manually handle
the configuration change,

177
00:12:36,910 --> 00:12:40,290
avoiding the whole shutdown and
restart sequence all together.

178
00:12:40,290 --> 00:12:42,320
Let's talk about each one
of those one at a time.

179
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:47,670
So to speed up reconfiguration, I said
you can cached hard to recompute data and

180
00:12:47,670 --> 00:12:49,770
store it in a Java object.

181
00:12:49,770 --> 00:12:55,490
And one way to do this is by overriding
the onRetainNonConfigurationInstance

182
00:12:55,490 --> 00:13:01,578
method, which will be called some
time between onStop and onDestroy.

183
00:13:01,578 --> 00:13:03,830
And the object that you build and

184
00:13:03,830 --> 00:13:08,672
return in onRetainNonConfigurationInstance
can be retrieved when

185
00:13:08,672 --> 00:13:14,020
the activity is recreated by calling
getLastNonConfigurationInstance.

186
00:13:14,020 --> 00:13:18,234
And that's usually done during the call to
the activity's onCreate method when you

187
00:13:18,234 --> 00:13:20,110
recreate the activity.

188
00:13:20,110 --> 00:13:23,390
Now, note that these methods
have been deprecated in favor of

189
00:13:23,390 --> 00:13:25,830
other methods in the Fragment class.

190
00:13:25,830 --> 00:13:28,130
And we haven't discussed fragments yet,
but

191
00:13:28,130 --> 00:13:30,460
we'll come back to it in
a later lesson when we do.

192
00:13:31,520 --> 00:13:35,190
Now, the second thing you can do to speed
up reconfiguration is to avoid the whole

193
00:13:35,190 --> 00:13:40,340
kill and restart sequence altogether for
specific configuration changes.

194
00:13:40,340 --> 00:13:44,093
And to do this, you declare
those specific changes that the,

195
00:13:44,093 --> 00:13:48,760
your activity will handle in
the AndroidManifest.xml file.

196
00:13:48,760 --> 00:13:54,070
For example, this XML snippet indicates
that MyActivity will manually

197
00:13:54,070 --> 00:13:59,310
handle changes in device orientation and
screen size and keyboard accessibility.

198
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:04,670
So, if you do handle some
configuration changes manually,

199
00:14:04,670 --> 00:14:07,705
then when those configuration
changes occur in runtime,

200
00:14:07,705 --> 00:14:11,709
your activity will receive a call
to onConfigurationChanged.

201
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:17,010
And as a parameter, this method
will receive a Configuration object

202
00:14:17,010 --> 00:14:19,510
which details the new configuration, so

203
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:23,200
your code can then read this object and
make whatever changes it needs to.
